Basic Installation
==================

These are generic build instructions.  AudioMove should be
buildable under any OS that has a valid Qt 5.x or Qt 6.x development
environment.

1) Make sure you have a Qt build environment set up on your machine.

2) Make sure you have the GNU autotools installed, since the bundled
   third-party libraries are built with them.  On MacOS with Homebrew:

     brew install qt autoconf automake libtool autogen pkg-config

   The "autogen" package is GNU AutoGen, which is separate from the
   autogen.sh scripts in the submodules.  libsndfile uses it to generate
   src/test_endswap.c, and its build will stop without it.

   On MacOS the GNU libtool is installed as "glibtoolize", so put
   Homebrew's gnubin directory ahead of it on your PATH before building:

     export PATH="$(brew --prefix libtool)/libexec/gnubin:$PATH"

3) If you downloaded this folder from GitHub (e.g. via a "git clone"
   command), be sure to do a

     git submodule update --init --recursive

   ... so that all the code for all the necessary third-party
   dependencies will be downloaded.

4) Run the included build script:

     source ./build-audiomove.sh

5) In a few minutes, your AudioMove executable should be built and
   ready in the audiomove sub-directory.


Notes for MacOS
===============

The build script pins the bundled C libraries to -std=gnu17.  Apple clang
16 and later default to -std=gnu23, in which an empty parameter list "f()"
declares a function taking no arguments rather than an unspecified number
of them; several of the bundled libraries still use K&R declarations and
will not compile as C23.

The script also builds the bundled libraries against a deployment target of
14.0 by default, to match the minimum OS of a Homebrew Qt 6 build.  Override
it if you are linking against a Qt that supports an older OS:

  MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=12.0 source ./build-audiomove.sh

To produce a redistributable .app that does not require Qt to be installed
on the target machine, run macdeployqt afterwards:

  macdeployqt audiomove/AudioMove.app -always-overwrite

It will report that it cannot resolve QtPdf, QtSvg and QtVirtualKeyboard.
Those are optional Qt modules that a qtbase-only install does not provide;
AudioMove does not use any of them and the resulting bundle runs fine.

Universal binaries
------------------

Setting BUILD_UNIVERSAL_BINARY builds the bundled libraries and AudioMove
for both x86_64 and arm64:

  BUILD_UNIVERSAL_BINARY=1 source ./build-audiomove.sh

Note that this only produces a working universal .app if the Qt you link
against is itself universal.  Homebrew's Qt is single-architecture (arm64
on Apple Silicon), so a universal build needs Qt from the official online
installer instead.
